{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788138722,"nonce":"h5cAk6vyk7KriI/eef3LaxSmgjM=","message":"WORKER RESOLUTIONS — 3.1, branch `spec/3.1-dev-verbs` (92a3347, 0f51078, 4d00d0b).\nSource-only; `dotnet build -c Release` clean; no DLL committed; the game was never\nlaunched. In-game acceptance is the orchestrator's.\n\n\nThe spec's twelve, all present: `dev:spawn-thing` `dev:spawn-pawn` `dev:heal`\n`dev:set-need` `dev:add-hediff` `dev:remove-hediff` `dev:set-skill`\n`dev:finish-research` `dev:incident` `dev:weather` `dev:destroy`\n`dev:starter-kit`.\n\nFive additions, each with the argument for it. I mined the whole DebugActions\ncatalog (DebugActionsMisc 72, DebugToolsPawns 104, DebugToolsSpawning 46,\nDebugToolsGeneral 45, DebugActionsMapManagement 45, DebugToolsMisc 27,\nDebugActionsIncidents 8, DebugTools_Health) and the bar for adding was **\"a\nfixture or an existing acceptance cannot be built without it\"**, not \"the game\nhas one\".\n\n- **`dev:unfog`** (DebugToolsGeneral.UnfogRect / FogGrid.ClearAllFog). The\n  strongest of the five. DESIGN's own decisions log makes fog binding on the\n  ENTIRE player-facing surface — map-view, find-rect, nearest, room-at, pawns,\n  pawn all hide fogged cells. `dev:*` is exempt, so a dev verb can happily stage\n  a fixture in ground the colony has never walked, and **every observer will\n  then report it as absent**. Without an unfog verb, this spec's own acceptance\n  bullet — \"spawned things visible to 2.x serializers\" — is not reliably\n  achievable for anything staged away from the colony, and the failure is\n  silent. It is one call (`map.fogGrid.Unfog(cell)`), and it is the verb that\n  makes the other sixteen observable.\n- **`dev:damage`** (HealthUtility.DamageUntilDowned / DamageUntilDead /\n  DamageLegsUntilIncapableOfMoving / DamageLimbsUntilIncapableOfManipulation,\n  plus DebugTools_Health.Options_Damage_BodyParts for the targeted case).\n  Supersedes `pawn-fixture wound` and `journal-selftest downed`. The\n  wounded-and-standing state 2.2's acceptance is written against has no other\n  route: `DamageUntilDowned` by definition cannot produce it. `mode:\"amount\"`\n  with `hits` stops the instant the pawn goes down, which is exactly what\n  pawn-fixture hand-rolled.\n- **`dev:mental-state`** (DebugToolsPawns.MentalState / StopMentalState).\n  Supersedes `journal-selftest break`. The journal's `mental_break` event type\n  has no other deterministic stimulus, and 5.1 will need one that is not a\n  selftest verb.\n- **`dev:guest-status`** (DebugToolsPawns.AddGuest(GuestStatus) -\u003e\n  Pawn_GuestTracker.SetGuestStatus). Supersedes `pawn-fixture prisoner`. Same\n  call JobDriver_TakeToBed's capture toil makes, so it produces a real prisoner\n  (resistance/will rolled, MapPawns re-registered, dynamic components rebuilt)\n  rather than a flag. 3.4 owns the player-facing warden verbs; this is the\n  fixture route and does not collide with them.\n- **`dev:faction-goodwill`** (Faction.TryAffectGoodwillWith /\n  SetRelationDirect; menu equivalent DebugActionsMisc.SetFactionRelations).\n  `dev:incident`'s most useful argument is `faction`, and DebugActionsIncidents\n  .DoRaid picks **RaidEnemy or RaidFriendly off `parms.faction.HostileTo`** —\n  so staging a hostile is a prerequisite of the raid fixture, not a nicety.\n  Factions is also the first mod under test (M3). Checked 3.2 and 3.4: neither\n  claims faction relations.\n\n**Deliberately NOT added**, so the boundary is on the record:\n\n- *A def-catalog / def-search verb.* Every dev verb's arguments are defNames and\n  there is no in-protocol way to discover them, which is a real gap — but 2.5\n  owns the catalog (`baseviz_catalog.json`), and a second def surface here would\n  be a second contract. Answered instead by making **every def miss list near\n  matches**: `no ThingDef named 'steel' (arg 'def') — did you mean: Steel,\n  SteelWall, ChunkSlagSteel…`. Same courtesy Poller already extends for\n  `unknown-op`. If the orchestrator finds that insufficient in practice, a def\n  verb belongs on 2.5, not here.\n- *`dev:tame` / `dev:recruit` / `dev:trait` / `dev:gene` / `dev:xenotype`.*\n  DESIGN's non-goals defer animal management and DLC-specific management; a\n  fixture verb for either would be building the surface those non-goals declined.\n- *`dev:time` / increment-season.* TimeDriver's `advance` already owns time.\n- *`dev:letter` / `dev:message` / `dev:error`.* Pure journal stimulus with no\n  fixture value; `journal-selftest` keeps them and stays for its own replay.\n- *`dev:push-heat` / `dev:explosion` / `dev:gas`.* Genuinely useful to\n  RealisticHeatDeath / SuperHotFire / RealisticGasses later, but no v1 fixture\n  needs them and each is a whole argument surface. Named here so a later wave\n  can pick them up rather than rediscover them.\n\nthe DebugAction chain does that a naive wrapper would miss\n\nExposed: `def` `points` `faction` `strategy` (RaidStrategyDef) `arrival`\n(PawnsArrivalModeDef) `at` + `drop_radius` (spawnCenter/dropInRadius, the\ndrop-pod-at-location shape) `trader_kind` `count` (the menu's \"Do incident x10\")\n`letter` (parms.sendLetter) `recount_wealth` `check_only`.\n\nNot exposed: `psychicRitualDef`, `mechClusterSketch`, `raidAgeRestriction`,\n`pawnGroups`, `quest*`, `gifts`, `attackTargets`. Each is one DLC's or one\nworker's private surface and none has a v1 fixture behind it.\n\nThree behaviours taken from DebugActionsIncidents rather than invented:\n\n1. **The pointsScaleable regenerate.** `GetIncidentDebugAction` does\n   `DefaultParmsNow` and then, if `def.pointsScaleable`, **throws it away and\n   regenerates through the storyteller's own OnOffCycle/RandomMain comp**. A\n   wrapper that only calls DefaultParmsNow gives a scaled incident a flat point\n   budget. Reproduced, and skipped when `points` is explicit.\n2. **`recount_wealth` defaults TRUE, and this is a correctness fix, not a\n   convenience.** A scaled incident's points come off the storyteller reading\n   `map.wealthWatcher`, which recounts on a slow tick. The acceptance sequence —\n   starter kit, then a scaled raid — would otherwise size the raid against the\n   colony's wealth **before** the kit, silently. `RecalculateThreatPoints` is\n   one call (`wealthWatcher.ForceRecount`) and makes the raid match the colony\n   you just built.\n3. **`can_fire_now` is reported before the attempt** and a refusal explains\n   which side said no, because `TryExecute` returning false is otherwise\n   indistinguishable between \"storyteller said no\" and \"no arrival spot\".\n\n**The 1.7 wedge (dispatch comment).** Answered three ways, and yes it is cheaply\ndetectable:\n\n- `letter_bearing` — the static prediction, from\n  `typeof(ChoiceLetter).IsAssignableFrom(def.letterDef.letterClass)`:\n  `\"choice\"` / `\"timeout\"` / `\"standard\"` / `false`. Cheap, but a HINT only: a\n  worker can raise a letter its def does not declare (quest offers do).\n- `letters_opened` — the letters that actually arrived, diffed off\n  `LetterStack.LettersListForReading` around the call, each flagged `choice`.\n- `force_pause_after` + `wedge_warning` — **measured**, in\n  `TimeDriver.ForcePausePayload`'s exact shape, whenever the window stack is\n  force-pausing after the call. The warning names the consequence (every later\n  advance halts `reason:\"dialog\"`, `OpenAutomaticLetters` dead for the session)\n  and the escape (`letter:false`).\n- `letter:false` sets `parms.sendLetter` and fires the incident silently, which\n  is the right default for an unattended fixture run and is what makes the\n  acceptance raid safe to script.\n\nThe same reasoning drove two suppressions elsewhere: `dev:finish-research`\npasses `doCompletionLetter:false` (the game's default is TRUE), and\n`dev:faction-goodwill` passes `canSendHostilityLetter:false`. Both defaults\nwould otherwise stack a modal mid-fixture.\n\nan autostart.rws\n\n```json\n{\"id\":\"kit\",\"op\":\"dev:starter-kit\",\"args\":{\n  \"preset\":\"survival\",\n  \"at\":\"base-center\",\n  \"stockpile\":true,\n  \"unfog\":25,\n  \"items\":[{\"def\":\"Steel\",\"count\":400,\"stuff\":null,\"quality\":\"Normal\"}],\n  \"extra_items\":[{\"def\":\"Gun_BoltActionRifle\",\"count\":3}],\n  \"pawns\":[{\"name\":\"Ada 'Ada' Rook\",\"kind\":\"Colonist\",\"faction\":\"player\",\n            \"skills\":{\"Construction\":10},\"passions\":{\"Construction\":\"Major\"},\n            \"needs\":{\"Food\":0.9},\"hediffs\":[]}],\n  \"research\":[\"Smithing\"],\n  \"save_as\":\"autostart\",\n  \"dry_run\":false}}\n```\n\nFour decisions behind that shape:\n\n- **No kit file on disk.** The protocol root's `config.json` belongs to 1.x\n  (`alertScanFrames`); a second config file is a second contract to keep in sync,\n  with no review trail and nothing to diff. A kit is a JSON document the caller\n  already has to write. Presets are code-side defaults it starts from.\n- **`save_as` is the point of the whole verb.** Spike 0.1's FINDINGS amendment\n  says the no-UI fixture path is `autostart.rws`\n  (`SaveGameFilesUtility.GetAutostartSaveFile` matches the file's stem,\n  lowercased, against `\"autostart\"`), so the config has to be complete enough\n  that one document IS the fixture definition and the last step writes the file.\n  `save_as:\"autostart\"` closes the loop; the result flags it.\n- **`dry_run` before `preset` discovery.** An unknown preset's error lists the\n  known ones; `dry_run:true` resolves the whole plan against the live\n  DefDatabase and mutates nothing. Discovery is through errors and dry runs,\n  which is the house style (`unknown-op` lists known ops) rather than a new\n  introspection verb.\n- **Merge rule, stated once:** a section the caller supplies REPLACES the\n  preset's section of that name; `extra_items` appends; nothing merges\n  element-wise. Element-wise list merging is where config formats go to die.\n\nPresets: `survival` (M1 baseline: food, meds, materials), `shelter`\n(construction stock only — the acceptance's \"shelter materials\"), `medical`,\n`combat`, `workshop` (materials + the early research that unlocks them). Every\ndefName checked against the bench's own `Data/Core/Defs`, not recalled —\n`Apparel_SimpleHelmet` exists, `Apparel_FlakHelmet` does not.\n\nThe kit runs the **same verb handlers** a caller would invoke one at a time\n(`DevVerbs.SpawnThing`, `DevPawnVerbs.SetSkill`, …) by building a VerbContext.\nOne implementation, one set of documented deviations, and per-ITEM provenance\nbecause each of those writes its own `dev` event. The kit's own line carries\n`caused_seqs`, so a post-mortem joins either direction.\n\n\nVerified end to end in code before committing, since the fallback was cheap and\na wrong guess here is a protocol-wide rename later:\n\n- `VerbAttribute` takes an arbitrary string; `VerbRegistry.RegisterAll` keys a\n  `Dictionary\u003cstring,VerbDef\u003e` on it with no sanitization.\n- `Poller.ScanInbox` reads `op` with `MiniJson.GetString` and uses it only to\n  look up the verb and to echo into the result envelope. It never reaches the\n  filesystem.\n- The result FILE is named from the command **id** — `Poller.ResultFileName(id)`\n  — and `Poller.Sanitize` is applied to that id alone. The op is not involved.\n- Client side: `rwa`'s `new_id(op)` already does\n  `re.sub(r'[^A-Za-z0-9]', '', op)[:12]`, so `dev:spawn-thing` yields the id\n  `devspawnthin-HHMMSS-PID`, which passes rwa's own `ID_RE`. And `rwa` carries no\n  verb table at all — `main()` sends any non-client word straight through as an\n  op — so `rwa dev:spawn-thing --def Steel` needs no client change.\n\nNothing in the chain sees the colon but the registry dictionary and the JSON\nenvelope. No fallback needed.\n\n\nThree classes of deliberate deviation, each named at its call site rather than\nleft as a surprise:\n\n1. **Determinism.** `DebugThingPlaceHelper.DebugSpawn` calls\n   `GenStuff.RandomStuffFor`, `QualityUtility.GenerateQualityRandomEqualChance`\n   and minifies anything `Minifiable`. A fixture that is not reproducible is not\n   a fixture, so the defaults are `DefaultStuffFor`, `Normal` and unminified;\n   `\"random\"` opts back in. (The minify default also matters on its own: the\n   DebugAction hands you a *minified* bed, which the 2.x serializers report as\n   an item, not a bed.) Same reasoning: `dev:spawn-pawn` defaults `spread:0`\n   (exactly at `pos`, the DebugAction's mouse cell has no fixture analogue) and\n   passes `forceGenerateNewPawn:true` so a fixture's roster does not depend on\n   world-pawn history.\n2. **Zero red errors.** Vanilla answers several bad calls with `Log.Error`,\n   which JournalHooks records as `red_error` against a standing invariant, and\n   the run is then stained with no link back to the verb that did it. Three\n   places reproduce the check instead: `GenSpawn.CanSpawnAt` before a direct\n   building spawn; `SetGuestStatus`'s two Guest-branch refusals (hostile pawn,\n   own faction) as clean bad-args; and the storage probe is **abandoned rather\n   than `Destroy()`ed**, because `Thing.Destroy` `Log.Error`s for a\n   `destroyable:false` def. Residual, and honestly flagged rather than papered\n   over: `GenPlace.TryPlaceThing` can still `Log.Error` if it cannot place at\n   all, and the result says `placed_is_floor` when anything failed.\n3. **A storage helper that NREs on the input we have.**\n   `StoreUtility.TryFindBestBetterStoreCellFor` with `carrier == null`\n   dereferences `carrier.PositionHeld` for any thing that is not yet spawned\n   (`TryFindBestBetterStoreCellForWorker`), and the thing we are about to spawn\n   never is. The two tests it ends in — `StorageSettings.AllowedToAccept` and\n   `StoreUtility.IsGoodStoreCell` — both handle `carrier == null` correctly, so\n   the walk is done here over the same `AllGroupsListInPriorityOrder`.\n\n\nDESIGN says dev verbs \"may bypass gates deliberately and say so\"; every result\ncarries a `dev` block:\n\n```json\n\"dev\":{\"cheat\":true,\"fog_exempt\":true,\"journal_seq\":118,\n       \"fogged\":[[71,140]],\"fogged_note\":\"acted on ground the colony has not explored…\"}\n```\n\n`fogged` is present ONLY when cells actually were fogged, so its presence is the\nsignal. `journal_seq` is the join key to this mutation's own journal line — it\nis what turns \"journal carries complete dev provenance\" into a checkable claim.\n`Journal.Emit` returns 0 when the writer is closed, and that is reported as\n`provenance:\"NOT WRITTEN…\"` rather than left to look like a normal result;\n`dry_run`/`check_only` get `journal_seq:null` with `\"not applicable\"`, because\n\"nothing to journal\" and \"failed to journal\" must not read the same.\n\n\n`journal-selftest` (1.2) and `pawn-fixture` (2.2) are superseded as stimulus but\n**not edited, not deleted** — they keep their own acceptance replays. Mapping:\nselftest `downed`-\u003e`dev:damage {mode:\"until-downed\"}`, `break`-\u003e`dev:mental-state`,\n`raid`-\u003e`dev:incident`; pawn-fixture `wound`-\u003e`dev:damage {mode:\"amount\"}`,\n`prisoner`-\u003e`dev:spawn-pawn`+`dev:guest-status`, `visitor`-\u003e`dev:incident\n{def:\"VisitorGroup\"}`. The one thing with no dev equivalent is pawn-fixture's\n`sadden` (stacking `ThoughtDefOf.DebugBad` memories): that is thought-system\nstimulus for a serializer test, not fixture staging. A fixture wants a mood\nVALUE, which `dev:set-need` gives — with the honest caveat that a `Need_Seeker`\ndrifts back, reported per call as `volatile:true` plus the rise/fall rates.\n\n\nChecked before writing: 3.2 owns designations/zones (I create no zones — the\nstarter kit places into EXISTING storage and scatters otherwise), 3.3 owns\nbuild/layout, 3.4 owns warden and pawn orders, 2.5 owns the def catalog. The\n`dev` journal event keeps the `{verb, step, target}` payload shape the two\nexisting writers use and only ADDS fields, which JOURNAL.md's \"consumers must\nignore unknown payload fields\" already licenses. No BLOCKED.","files":null}]}